{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And they took him.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that Shimon took him, and therefore it is written (Genesis 42:24): ״ויקח מאתם את שמעון״ — and he took Shimon from them." "<b>And the pit was empty; there was no water in it.</b> Even if it had a little water in it, it would still be called 'empty.' Therefore, when Scripture says ״אין בו מים״ — there was no water in it — the intent is that there was no water in it at all." "<b>And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted:</b> 'Water it did not have, but snakes and scorpions it did have.' And the Sages needed to say this because there are two terms of exclusion in the verse: ״והבור רק״ — 'and the pit was empty' — is one exclusion; ״אין בו מים״ — 'there was no water in it' — is a second exclusion. And one exclusion after another exclusion serves only to include, and it comes to include something harmful — and that is snakes and scorpions." "<b>And Rabbenu Tam</b>, of blessed memory, explained that this reached our Rabbis of blessed memory by way of an inference from the verse ״אין בו מים״ — from what is written in the wilderness (Deuteronomy 8:15): ״וצמאון אשר אין מים״ — 'and thirst where there was no water' — and there were snakes there, as it is written: ״נחש שרף ועקרב״. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote: there were snakes in the borings of the pit, but they did not see them at all. For had they seen them and they did not harm him, it would have become clear to them that a miracle was being performed for him and that his merit would save him from them; and how could they touch the anointed of HaShem, in whom He delights and whom He saves, in the manner of what is written (Daniel 6:23): ״אלהי שלח מלאכיה וסגר פום אריותא ולא חבלוני״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'And the pit was empty' — the pit of Jacob was emptied of his sons, but they did not know the Torah, as it is said: 'there was no water in it' — there were no words of Torah among them, for Torah is compared to water, as it is said (Isaiah 55:1): ״הוי כל צמא לכו למים״. And they did not know what is written in the Torah (Deuteronomy 24:7): ״כי ימצא איש גונב נפש מאחיו מבני ישראל והתעמר בו ומכרו ומת הגנב ההוא״ — and they sold their brother."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {0 "The printed citation reads 'בראשית מלכים ב כ״ד:י״ב' which is corrupt; the prooftext is Genesis 42:24. Corrected accordingly.", 3 "Read 'בחורי הבור' hyper-literally as 'in the borings of the pit'; the phrase is unusual and may be textually uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:27.909874358Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}